===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=noun|:inh|enm:hond|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|hond}}, {{m|enm|hand}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hand}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*handu}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*handuz}}.

See also {{cog|nl|-}} and  {{cog|sv|hand|t=hand}}, {{cog|da|hånd}}, {{cog|de|Hand}}, {{cog|fy|hân}}. Perhaps compare Old Swedish {{m|sv|hinna||to gain}}, {{cog|got|𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌰𐌽|𐍆𐍂𐌰-𐌷𐌹𐌽𐌸𐌰𐌽|to take captive, capture}}; and {{cog|lv|sīts||hunting spear}}, {{cog|grc|κεντέω||prick}}, {{cog|sq|çandër||pitchfork, prop}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The [[part]] of the [[forelimb]] below the [[forearm]] or [[wrist]] in humans and other [[primate|primates]], and the corresponding part in many other [[animal]]s.
#: {{meronyms|en|index finger|middle finger|palm|pinky|ring finger|thumb}}
#: {{ux|en|Her '''hands''' are really strong.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=7|passage=I made a speaking trumpet of my '''hands''' and commenced to whoop “Ahoy!” and “Hello!” at the top of my lungs. {{...}} The Colonel woke up, and, after asking what in brimstone was the matter, opened his mouth and roared “Hi!” and “Hello!” like the bull of Bashan.}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=26 December 2012|author=[[w:John Branch (journalist)|John Branch]]|title=Snow Fall: The Avalanche at Tunnel Creek|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20131114133825/http://www.nytimes.com/projects/2012/snow-fall/?smid=fb-nytimes&WT.z_sma=SP_SNT_20130415#/?part=tunnel-creek|passage=Using her '''hands''' like windshield wipers, she tried to flick snow away from her mouth. When she clawed at her chest and neck, the crumbs maddeningly slid back onto her face. She grew claustrophobic.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=Anti-Sub Warfare in WW1 - From Hammers to Hunter-Killers|date=24 July 2019|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lxV9fQO4O1U|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221124003343/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lxV9fQO4O1U|archivedate=24 November 2022|accessdate=11 September 2023|time=11:06|text=The fruits of these kickstarted endeavors began to show in 1915, first in the deployment of a new range of depth charges.  These were, mercifully, smaller than Jellicoe's "crushing '''hand''' of God" prototype, and, whilst practically just as lethal to submarines, they were ''significantly'' less risky to the launching ship, and could also be carried in larger numbers.}}
# ''That which resembles, or to some extent performs the office of, a human hand.''
## A limb of certain animals, such as the [[foot]] of a hawk, or any one of the four extremities of a monkey.
## An index or [[pointer]] on a dial; such as the hour and minute hands on the [[face]] of an analog [[clock]], which are used to indicate the [[time]] of day.
