# {{l|en|hand}}
#* [https://books.google.com/books?id=9ZOeBwAAQBAJ&pg=PA23 Monastic sign language guide]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ðonne þū fisc habban wylle, þonne weġe þū þīne '''hand''', swilċe he dēþ his tæġl þonne hē swymð.|If you want to have a fish, then move your '''hand''', like it (a fish) does with its tail when it swims.}}
#* [https://books.google.com/books?id=9ZOeBwAAQBAJ&pg=PA23 Monastic sign language guide]
#*: {{quote|ang|Þonne þū sāpan habban wille, þonne gnīd þū þīne '''handa''' tōgædere.|When you want to have soap, rub your '''hands''' together.}}
#* [https://books.google.com/books?id=9ZOeBwAAQBAJ&pg=PA23 Monastic sign language guide]
#*: {{quote|ang|Brōca tācn is þæt þū strīċe mid þīnum twām '''handum''' upp on þīn þēoh.|The sign for underwear is that you stroke up your thigh with both '''hands'''.}}
#* [[w:en:Fonthill Letter|The Fonthill Letter]]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ða næs Æðelm na fullice geðafa ær we eodan in to cinge ⁊ sædan eall hu we hit reahtan ⁊ be hwy we hit reahtan. ⁊ Æðelm stod self ðærinne mid, ⁊ cing stod ðwoh his '''honda''' æt Weardoran innan ðon bure.|Then Æthelm wasn’t fully in agreement before we went in (to see) the king, and talked all about how we explained it, and why we explained it. And Æthelm stood by himself therein, and (the) king stood washing his '''hands''' at Weardoran within that room.}}
#* {{quote-book|ang|text=''Oft him ānhaga ·  āre gebīdeð, <br/>Metudes miltse, · þēah þe hē mōdċeariġ<br/>ġeond lagulāde · longe sċeolde <br/>hrēran mid '''hondum''' · hrīmċealde sǣ,<br/>wadan wræclāstas. · Wyrd bið ful ārǣd.''|t=A loner oft waits a grace for himself, <br/>Creator's mercy, even if he is sorrowful,<br/>through a sea-way he should for long<br/>stir the frost-cold sea with '''hands''',<br/>travel paths of exile. Fate is well stalwart.|title=The Wanderer|year=10th century}}
#* {{RQ:ang:Herzfeld Martyrology|page=152|text=Ond þær him com to Godes engel ond ætywde þære þeode hwæt hyra god wæs þe hi ær beeodon. He him ætywde micelne Sigelhearwan, þæm wæs seo onsyn sweartre þonne hrum, ond se beard ond þær feax him wæron oð þa fet side, ond þa eagan wæron swylce fyren iren, ond him sprungon spearcan of þam muðe, and ful rec him eode of þæm næsþyrlum, ond he hæfde fiðru swylce þyrnen besma, ond þa '''handa''' wæron gebunden tosomne mid fyrenum racentum, ond he hrymde mid grimlicre stefne ond ladlicre ond fleah aweg ond nahwær siððan ætywde. Þæt wæs þæt deofol þæt seo þeod hyre ær for god beeodon, ond hi nemdon þone Astaroð.|t=And an angel of God came to him there and revealed to the people who their god was whom they had formerly worshipped. He showed them a big Ethiopian, whose face was blacker than soot, his beard and hair were down to his feet, the eyes were like glowing iron, sparks sprang forth from the mouth, foul smoke came out of his nostrils, he had wings like a broom of thorns, and the '''hands''' were bound together with fiery chains: he cried out with a fierce and horrible voice and flew away and did not appear anywhere afterwards. That was the devil whom the people formerly had honoured as a god, and they called him Astaroth.}}
# [[side]] {{gloss|of a conflict}}
#* {{RQ:ang:Thorpe Chronicle|page=236|entry=982|text=⁊ þȳ ilcan ġēare fōr Odda Rōmana cāsere tō Grēclande, ⁊ þā ġemette hē þāra Sarcena myċele fyrde cuman upp of sǣ, ⁊ woldon þā faran on hergoð on þæt Crīstene folc; ⁊ þā ġefeaht þā cāsere wið hī. ⁊ þǣr wæs myċel wæl ġeslæġen on ġehwæþere '''hand''', ⁊ sē cāsere āhte wælstōwe ġeweald. ⁊ hwæðere hē þǣr wæs miċċlum ġeswenced, ǣr hē þanon hwurfe, ⁊ þā hē hāmweard fōr. ⁊ hē wæs Lēodulfes sunu æþelinges, ⁊ sē Lēodulf wæs þǣs ealdan Oddan sunu, ⁊ Ēadweardes cynginges dohtor sunu.|t=And in the same year, Emperor Otto of Rome went to Greece, and there he encountered a great army of Saracens who had come up from the sea, and intended to then go make war against the Christian people; and so the emperor then fought against them. There was great slaughter on each '''side''', and the emperor took control of the battlefield. Nevertheless, he was very fatigued, and so thence turned around and traveled towards home. He was the son of Prince Leodulf, and Leodulf was son of the elder Otto, and the son of King Edward's daughter.}}

